ses the original prompt plus the proposed tokens in a single forward pass and computes its own probabilities for every position in that sequence. This lets the target model verify multiple candidate tokens at once instead of generating them one by one, which is the usual bottleneck in autoregressive decoding.\n\nThe key trick is a rejection test that preserves the target model\u2019s output distribution. For each proposed token, the method compares the target probability with the draft probability. If the target model strongly agrees, the token is accepted. If it disagrees, the token is rejected and a new token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s probabilities while accounting for the draft\u2019s proposal. Because this correction is mathematically equivalent to sampling from the target model alone, the final text has the same distribution as normal decoding, even though some tokens were produced by the draft.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how often the draft model\u2019s proposals are accepted, the cost of running the draft model, the number of tokens proposed per step, and the hardware\u2019s ability to exploit the target model\u2019s parallel verification. If the draft is too small or poorly aligned, many proposals are rejected; if it is too large, drafting costs erode the gain. The two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft-target agreement on the task or domain, and overhead from running the draft model or handling rejected tokens that cancels the benefit of", "top2": [[[1596, -0.002238153712823987], [760, -6.937777519226074]], [[1144, -1.406679530191468e-05], [4087, -11.68938159942627]], [[4087, -0.15792371332645416], [310, -1.9319506883621216]], [[1156, -0.006726725492626429], [303, -5.050220012664795]], [[579, -0.25706779956817627], [25, -1.783070683479309]], [[1622, -0.011149341240525246], [3296, -4.841509819030762]], [[25, -0.3104989528656006], [13, -1.358108401298523]], [[59987, -0